Blood is a reusable fld::blood profile layered on fld::continuum.
It does not create a second fluid solver, collision system, particle system,
or pressure grid.

Files:
  blood.hpp, blood.cpp                  blood material and reusable adapter
  ../continuum/shaders/grid.comp        optional Carreau shear thinning and floor drag
  ../continuum/shaders/surface.comp     FLD_BLOOD optical shading (same renderer)

Usage:
  fld::blood::material profile{};
  profile.viscosity_zero=.25f;
  profile.viscosity_high=.0035f;
  profile.relaxation_seconds=25.f;
  profile.shear_exponent=.25f;
  profile.substrate_drag=4.f;
  profile.shallow_color={.71f,.046f,.038f};
  profile.optical_density=32.f;
  auto programs=fld::blood::shaders("build/shaders/");
  fld::blood::init(options,drop,programs,frames,width,height,profile);
  fld::blood::record(command,frame,dt,render_input);
  fld::blood::shutdown();

Material units: density kg/m^3, viscosity Pa*s, surface tension N/m,
relaxation s, contact drag 1/s. The example uses approximately 1060 kg/m^3,
Carreau mu_0=.25 Pa*s, mu_infinity=.0035 Pa*s, lambda=25 s, n=.25.
These are illustrative blood rheology values; hematocrit and temperature
change actual blood properties. The extra contact drag is a game-look
approximation, not an intrinsic medical parameter.

The optical layer combines transmitted background with red-selective
absorption. Thin layers are brighter and more translucent; thicker layers
become darker, more opaque burgundy. Colors and optical density are editable
in the blood material. Water shaders remain available as the normal
continuum rendering variant.

Not simulated: clots, drying, cellular blood flow, fine splashes, true
contact-angle hysteresis or medically validated rheology. This is a
single-fluid real-time approximation with numerical limits of the existing
VOF/MAC pressure solver. GPU runtime and 60 FPS are not yet validated.
